+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes),
+and `s` (seconds). Each unit may appear at most once and in that order, with no spaces. Units are case-insensitive, and
+a unit's value isn't limited by the next larger unit, so `90m` is valid.
+
+For example:
+
+- `120` is `timedelta(seconds=120)`.
+- `2d` is `timedelta(days=2)`.
+- `1h30m` is `timedelta(hours=1, minutes=30)`.
+- `45S` is `timedelta(seconds=45)`.
+- `1d2h3m4s` is `timedelta(days=1, hours=2, minutes=3, seconds=4)`.
+
+Negative values, decimals, unknown units, repeated or out-of-order units, and spaces are not accepted. A
+{class}`~datetime.timedelta` passed as a default or from Python is returned unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
